<UserControl x:Class="EmailClient.UI.RecipientBox"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False">
    <!--
    A Gmail/Outlook-style recipient chip field, replacing what used to be a plain TextBox holding
    raw "Name <address@x.com>, Name2 <address2@x.com>" text — correct as the wire format, but not
    something a person should have to read while composing. Each committed recipient becomes a
    rounded pill showing just their name (the address moves to a tooltip); the one thing still
    being typed lives in a plain inline TextBox at the end, which is also what the recipient
    autocomplete popup anchors to.

    Deliberately built as raw children of an ItemsControl-hosted WrapPanel rather than binding an
    ItemsSource + DataTemplate — the "N chips, then one live TextBox, all wrapping together"
    element is a mixed collection an ItemsSource can't express cleanly, and directly managing
    children in code-behind is the same pattern this app's other dynamic popups (the colour
    picker, the contacts picker) already use.
    -->
    <!-- Cursor="IBeam" here, not just on the input TextBox — the Border spans the field's whole
         hit-test area, and WPF only shows the text caret cursor automatically over an actual
         TextBox's own content region, same reasoning as the plain fields' own I-beam fix. Without
         it, everywhere except directly over the small live-typing TextBox showed a plain arrow.
         VerticalAlignment="Center": a WrapPanel lays its content out from the top and never
         centers it within extra space on its own, so once this control stretches to fill the
         field row's full MinHeight, the chips/caret would otherwise sit flush at the top of that
         row instead of centered in it. -->
    <Border x:Name="Root" Background="Transparent" MinHeight="24" Cursor="IBeam"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            MouseLeftButtonDown="Root_MouseLeftButtonDown">
        <ItemsControl x:Name="Chips" Focusable="False">
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <WrapPanel Orientation="Horizontal"/>
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
        </ItemsControl>
    </Border>
</UserControl>
